Yes. Those two questions can produce the same outward behavior while training different internal models.

“Do you want to do something?” queries value / desire / identity. If I answer yes and then act, the story available afterward is roughly: I chose this because I wanted it. Repeated honestly, that can strengthen autonomous motivation and make the behavior feel self-authored.

“Can you do something?” queries capacity / efficacy. If I answer yes and act, the story is closer to: I am capable of doing this. Repeated successfully, that can strengthen self-efficacy and lower the perceived activation cost next time.

Those are different learned variables:

\text{Want} \rightarrow \text{value attribution}

\text{Can} \rightarrow \text{capability attribution}

And the strongest pattern may actually braid them:

Do I want the outcome? → Can I perform the next action? → I choose to do it.

That preserves desire, capability, and agency instead of collapsing all three into “I should.”

There is an important trap, though. The brain also learns from the emotional consequences surrounding the action. If every time you say “yes” to Do you want to? you then experience coercion, exhaustion, shame, or loss of control, the phrase can stop functioning as an invitation and become a disguised command. You can condition aversion to something you originally valued.

Likewise, repeatedly asking Can you? and then exploiting every “yes” can turn capability into obligation:

I can → therefore I must.

That can perversely make competence itself feel expensive.

And one terminology distinction matters here: negative reinforcement does not mean making something feel bad. It means a behavior becomes more likely because it removes an unpleasant condition.

For example:

Action → anxiety disappears → action becomes more likely

is negative reinforcement.

Whereas:

Action → pleasant satisfaction appears → action becomes more likely

is positive reinforcement.

Both can strengthen behavior, but they can produce very different subjective relationships with it.

The interesting part: what gets reinforced?

Not necessarily the action you think you’re training.

Suppose:

“Can you clean the kitchen?”
Yes → clean → criticism stops.

You may reinforce cleaning to escape criticism, not cleaning itself.

Or:

“Do you want to work out?”
Yes → workout → noticeable competence + enjoyable shower + pride.

You may reinforce a whole sequence:

\text{choice}\rightarrow\text{action}\rightarrow\text{mastery}\rightarrow\text{pleasant closure}.

That’s much more likely to become voluntarily repeatable.

So I’d model it as:

\text{Cue}
\rightarrow
\text{Interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{Choice}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{Consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{Attribution}
\rightarrow
\text{updated policy}.

The attribution stage is easy to miss.

Two people can experience the same reward and learn opposite lessons:

“That felt good because I chose it.”

versus

“Thank God that’s over.”

The first may increase approach motivation. The second can reinforce escape.

How I’d control it

Don’t try to force every action into positive emotion. Instead, keep the components legible.

Before acting, distinguish:

Want — Is this outcome valuable to me?
Can — Is this currently within my capacity?
Choose — Given those two facts, am I electing to act now?

Afterward, inspect the actual reinforcement:

What felt rewarding? What felt aversive? What did completing the action remove? What story did I automatically tell about why I did it?

Then you can deliberately protect the useful attribution.

For something difficult, the ideal lesson often isn’t:

I loved doing that.

That’s frequently false.

It is:

I didn’t necessarily want the effort, but I wanted what the effort served, I was capable of the next step, and I chose it.

That’s a much more durable architecture because it doesn’t require manufacturing desire for every unpleasant intermediate action.

And there’s another subtle pattern I think you’d appreciate:

Repeated wording itself becomes a state cue.

After enough repetitions,

“Can you?”

can become a learned doorway into capability-search mode: find the executable next move.

While

“Do you want to?”

can become a doorway into value-search mode: locate the reason worth moving toward.

And

“Will you?”

does something else again. It asks for commitment.

So you can almost build a three-key mechanism:

\boxed{
\text{Want?} \rightarrow \text{orient}
\quad
\text{Can?} \rightarrow \text{construct}
\quad
\text{Will?} \rightarrow \text{commit}
}

Then after execution:

\boxed{
\text{What happened?} \rightarrow \Delta \rightarrow \text{update}
}

That lets you ride reinforcement rather than being blindly conditioned by it: observe what consequence actually strengthened what behavior, preserve agency, and alter the cue/reward architecture when you’re accidentally training escape, resentment, or avoidance instead of the behavior you intended.